{"abstract":"Aims: To assess whether lifestyle factors, including sleep pattern, are predictors for the development of functional somatic disorder (FSD). Methods: A population-based prospective cohort of 9656 men and women aged 18–76 years was established in 2011–2015 and invited for re-examination in 2017–2020, when 5738 participated. Median follow-up period was 65 months. Participants filled in validated questionnaires on lifestyle, sleep pattern and various delimitations of FSD, which were operationalized using two different approaches: bodily distress syndrome (BDS) and functional somatic syndromes (FSS) (i.e. chronic fatigue, chronic widespread pain (CWP), irritable bowel, and multiple chemical sensitivity (MCS)). Baseline lifestyle and sleep pattern in relation to incidence of BDS and FSS (chronic fatigue, CWP, irritable bowel, MCS) was analysed by logistic regressions, adjusted for age, sex and subjective social status. Results: Inferior sleep quality at baseline predicted both incidence of BDS and all FSS delimitations except MCS. Smoking, alcohol intake, and low physical activity, but not diet, were predictors for the development of BDS. No uniform pattern was observed for the FSS. Smoking predicted development of chronic fatigue, CWP and irritable bowel, but not MCS. Alcohol and food quality only influenced the development of chronic fatigue whereas low physical activity only influenced the development of chronic fatigue and CWP. Conclusions: Lifestyle factors and sleep pattern seem to be predictors for some delimitations of FSD, but the importance of the various lifestyle factors is different for the different delimitations. The study shows the importance of analysing the various FSSs separately.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Torben","Jacobsen, Rikke A.","Petersen, Marie Weinreich","Ahrendt Bjerregaard, Anne","Schovsbo, Signe Ulfbeck","Gormsen, Lise Kirstine","Falgaard Eplov, Lene","Linneberg, Allan","Fink, Per","Benros, Michael Eriksen","Dantoft, Thomas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-772938"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Jørgensen, Torben"],"keyword":["Epidemiology","Functional somatic disorder","Incidence","Lifestyle","Sleep pattern"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"lifestyle factors as predictors of incident functional somatic disorder five year follow up of the danfund study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85207139180","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-772938","identifier":[{"id":"39439276","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85207139180","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-875c978c-9dcb-4fc5-9701-d9babbca64f2"],"keyword":["Functional somatic disorder","epidemiology","incidence","lifestyle","sleep pattern"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2D07CFD36BEA6BAC904780595BFB9339"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Clinic for Functional Disorder","Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Research clinic for gambling addiction"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Torben","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Torben","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rikke A.","key":"jacobsenpau","last":"Jacobsen","name":"Jacobsen, Rikke A.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Marie Weinreich","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"9dacce30-6a88-4fa2-a71a-87cccaf540af","name":"Petersen, Marie Weinreich","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anne","key":"ahrendtbjerregaardpau","last":"Ahrendt Bjerregaard","name":"Ahrendt Bjerregaard, Anne","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Signe Ulfbeck","key":"schovsbopau","last":"Schovsbo","name":"Schovsbo, Signe Ulfbeck","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lise Kirstine","key":"gormsenpau","last":"Gormsen","locid":"5516e253-cc95-468b-b53d-b6d1758f9884","name":"Gormsen, Lise Kirstine","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"falgaardeplovpau","last":"Falgaard Eplov","name":"Falgaard Eplov, Lene","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Allan","key":"linnebergpau","last":"Linneberg","name":"Linneberg, Allan","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Per Klausen","key":"finkpau","last":"Fink","locid":"22d646ca-5dee-456c-ae39-6c0687a18ce8","name":"Fink, Per","name_org":"Fink, Per Klausen","orcid":"0000-0003-2921-4099","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael Eriksen","key":"benrospau","last":"Benros","name":"Benros, Michael Eriksen","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"dantoftpau","last":"Dantoft","name":"Dantoft, Thomas","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39439276"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/14034948241283545","issn":["18778860","18778879"],"issn_org":["18778860"],"journal":"Scandinavian Journal of Pain","pages":"1-10","publisher":"Walter de Gruyter GmbH","status":"e","title":"Scandinavian Journal of Pain"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85207139180"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["E-Published (ahead of print)"]},"sid":["au-875c978c-9dcb-4fc5-9701-d9babbca64f2"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Lifestyle factors as predictors of incident functional somatic disorder. Five-year follow-up of The DanFunD study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-772938","year":"2024","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"We present a general multi-fidelity (MF) framework which is applied through utilizing flexible-order explicit finite difference numerical schemes using convolutional neural networks (CNNs) by combining low-order simulation data with higher order simulation data obtained from numerical simulations based on partial differential equations (PDEs). This allows for improving the performance of low-order numerical simulation through learning from the data how to correct the numerical schemes to achieve improved accuracy. Through the lens of numerical analysis we evaluate the accuracy, efficiency and generalizability of constructed data-driven MF-models. To illustrate the concept, the construction of the MF models uses CNNs and is evaluated against numerical schemes designed for solving linear PDEs; the heat, the linear advection equation and linearized 1D shallow water equations. The numerical schemes allow for a high level of explainability of data-driven correction terms obtained via CNNs through numerical analysis of truncation errors. It is demonstrated that data-driven MF models is a means to improve the accuracy of LF models through operator correction.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Petersen, Freja T,","Engsig-Karup, Allan Peter"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-789062"],"event":{"end":"2024-05-11","place":"Vienna, at","start":"2024-05-11","title":"ICLR 2024 Workshop on AI4DifferentialEquations in Science","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"data driven multi fidelity modelling for time dependent partial differential equations using convolutional neural networks","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/360075120/24_data_driven_multi_fidelity_mod.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-789062","ids":["dtu-758d71ba-1d1a-46ae-99cc-5dbd8dfef5b2"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-D3FF7B0A0DFD436B0C5FBAA14DF679CB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/360075120/24_data_driven_multi_fidelity_mod.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=8SvMFLOXvf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Scientific Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Freja T,","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","name":"Petersen, Freja T,","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Allan P.","key":"engsigkaruppau","last":"Engsig-Karup","locid":"f2fda3c5-b5fe-40c8-9cf4-e2bab14c176b","name":"Engsig-Karup, Allan Peter","name_org":"Engsig-Karup, Allan P.","orcid":"0000-0001-8626-1575","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ICLR 2024 Workshop on AI4DifferentialEquations in Science"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-758d71ba-1d1a-46ae-99cc-5dbd8dfef5b2"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Data-driven Multi-Fidelity Modelling for Time-dependent Partial Differential Equations using Convolutional Neural Networks","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-789062","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Peatlands cover only 3–4% of the Earth’s surface, but they store nearly 30% of global soil carbon stock. This significant carbon store is under threat as peatlands continue to be degraded at alarming rates around the world. It has prompted countries worldwide to establish regulations to conserve and reduce emissions from this carbon rich ecosystem. For example, the EU has implemented new rules that mandate sustainable management of peatlands, critical to reaching the goal of carbon neutrality by 2050. However, a lack of information on the extent and condition of peatlands has hindered the development of national policies and restoration efforts. This paper reviews the current state of knowledge on mapping and monitoring peatlands from field sites to the globe and identifies areas where further research is needed. It presents an overview of the different methodologies used to map peatlands in nine countries, which vary in definition of peat soil and peatland, mapping coverage, and mapping detail. Whereas mapping peatlands across the world with only one approach is hardly possible, the paper highlights the need for more consistent approaches within regions having comparable peatland types and climates to inform their protection and urgent restoration. The review further summarises various approaches used for monitoring peatland conditions and functions. These include monitoring at the plot scale for degree of humification and stoichiometric ratio, and proximal sensing such as gamma radiometrics and electromagnetic induction at the field to landscape scale for mapping peat thickness and identifying hotspots for greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions. Remote sensing techniques with passive and active sensors at regional to national scale can help in monitoring subsidence rate, water table, peat moisture, landslides, and GHG emissions. Although the use of water table depth as a proxy for interannual GHG emissions from peatlands has been well established, there is no single remote sensing method or data product yet that has been verified beyond local or regional scales. Broader land-use change and fire monitoring at a global scale may further assist national GHG inventory reporting. Monitoring of peatland conditions to evaluate the success of individual restoration schemes still requires field work to assess local proxies combined with remote sensing and modeling. Long-term monitoring is necessary to draw valid conclusions on revegetation outcomes and associated GHG emissions in rewetted peatlands, as their dynamics are not fully understood at the site level. Monitoring vegetation development and hydrology of restored peatlands is needed as a proxy to assess the return of water and changes in nutrient cycling and biodiversity.","author_count":43,"contributors":["Minasny, Budiman","Adetsu, Diana Vigah","Aitkenhead, Matt","Artz, Rebekka R.E.","Baggaley, Nikki","Barthelmes, Alexandra","Beucher, Amélie Marie","Caron, Jean","Conchedda, Giulia","Connolly, John","Deragon, Raphaël","Evans, Chris","Fadnes, Kjetil","Fiantis, Dian","Gagkas, Zisis","Gilet, Louis","Gimona, Alessandro","Glatzel, Stephan","Greve, Mogens Humlekrog","Habib, Wahaj","Hergoualc’h, Kristell","Hermansen, Cecilie","Kidd, Darren B.","Koganti, Triven","Kopansky, Dianna","Large, David J.","Larmola, Tuula","Lilly, Allan","Liu, Haojie","Marcus, Matthew","Middleton, Maarit","Morrison, Keith","Petersen, Rasmus Jes","Quaife, Tristan","Rochefort, Line","Rudiyanto, no-firstname","Toca, Linda","Tubiello, Francesco N.","Weber, Peter Lystbæk","Weldon, Simon","Widyatmanti, Wirastuti","Williamson, Jenny","Zak, Dominik Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-708821"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, South","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Minasny, Budiman"],"country":["Australia","Austria","Canada","Finland","France","Germany","Indonesi