ank":13,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3586579","issn":["26378051"],"issue":"2","journal":"Acm Transactions on Computing and Healthcare","paperid":"13","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Acm Transactions on Computing and Healthcare","title_org":"ACM Transactions on Computing for Healthcare","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85132030925"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-ffd84872-1074-4309-9471-099d42eccdb9","ku-4b80de00-49db-40df-bbf4-cb3a33777931"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"DiaFocus : A Personal Health Technology for Adaptive Assessment in Long-Term Management of Type 2 Diabetes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-088.49-673000","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Creatine transporter deficiency (CTD), caused by pathogenic variants in SLC6A8, is the second most common cause of X-linked intellectual disability. Symptoms include intellectual disability, epilepsy, and behavioral disorders and are caused by reduced cerebral creatine levels. Targeted treatment with oral supplementation is available, however the treatment efficacy is still being investigated. There are clinical and theoretical indications that heterozygous females with CTD respond better to supplementation treatment than hemizygous males. Unfortunately, heterozygous females with CTD often have more subtle and uncharacteristic clinical and biochemical phenotypes, rendering diagnosis more difficult. We report a new female case who presented with learning disabilities and seizures. After determining the diagnosis with molecular genetic testing confirmed by proton magnetic resonance spectroscopy (1H-MRS), the patient was treated with supplementation treatment including creatine, arginine, and glycine. After 28 months of treatment, the patient showed prominent clinical improvement and increased creatine levels in the brain. Furthermore, we provide a review of the 32 female cases reported in the current literature including a description of phenotypes, genotypes, diagnostic approaches, and effects of supplementation treatment. Based on this, we find that supplementation treatment should be tested in heterozygous female patients with CTD, and a prospective treatment underlines the importance of diagnosing these patients. The diagnosis should be suspected in a broad clinical spectrum of female patients and can only be made by molecular genetic testing. 1H-MRS of cerebral creatine levels is essential for establishing the diagnosis in females, and especially valuable when assessing variants of unknown significance.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Mejdahl Nielsen, Malene","Petersen, Esben Thade","Fenger, Christina Dühring","Ørngreen, Mette Cathrine","Siebner, Hartwig Roman","Boer, Vincent Oltman","Považan, Michal","Lund, Allan","Grønborg, Sabine Weller","Hammer, Trine Bjørg"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-716219"],"filters":{"added":"2023-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Mejdahl Nielsen, Malene"],"keyword":["Creatine transporter deficiency","Female case report","Proton Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy","SLC6A8","Supplementation treatment","X linked intellectual disability"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-FILA"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-FILA","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"x linked creatine transporter slc000000000006a000000000008 deficiency in females difficult to recognize but a potentially treatable disease","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ymgme.2023.107694","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Hartwig R. Siebner holds a 5-year professorship in precision medicine at the Faculty of Health Sciences and Medicine , University of Copenhagen which is sponsored by the Lundbeck Foundation (Grant No. R186-2015-2138 )."],"id":"S02-100.00-716219","identifier":[{"id":"37708665","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85170676868","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-4523a15a-d037-4725-bf09-14abb595d1bf","ku-446e12eb-386e-4069-bcbd-53d5de0d245a"],"keyword":["Creatine transporter deficiency","Female case report","Proton magnetic resonance spectroscopy","SLC6A8","Supplementation treatment","X-linked intellectual disability"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-967CB5EE02F02641A5