,"rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Torben","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"6b6bfff1-8b14-468b-af77-e71b375018e0","name":"Hansen, Torben","orcid":"0000-0001-8748-3831","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29926116"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00125-018-4659-2","issn":["0012186X","14320428"],"issn_org":["0012186X"],"issue":"9","journal":"Diabetologia","pages":"2005-2015","publisher":"Springer Science+Business Media","status":"p","title":"Diabetologia","volume":"61"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85048749142"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-9d19baa6-9a1c-4faf-874a-841e03798d04","sdu-d93572d6-16c8-472c-a49a-26891b05fbb6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Identification of novel high-impact recessively inherited type 2 diabetes risk variants in the Greenlandic population","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.95-044761","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"The 6%–9% risk of an untoward outcome previously established by Warburton for prenatally detected de novo balanced chromosomal rearrangements (BCRs) does not account for long-term morbidity. We performed long-term follow-up (mean 17 years) of a registry-based nationwide cohort of 41 individuals carrying a prenatally detected de novo BCR with normal first trimester screening/ultrasound scan. We observed a significantly higher frequency of neurodevelopmental and/or neuropsychiatric disorders than in a matched control group (19.5% versus 8.3%, p = 0.04), which was increased to 26.8% upon clinical follow-up. Chromosomal microarray of 32 carriers revealed no pathogenic imbalances, illustrating a low prognostic value when fetal ultrasound scan is normal. In contrast, mate-pair sequencing revealed disrupted genes (ARID1B, NPAS3, CELF4), regulatory domains of known developmental genes (ZEB2, HOXC), and complex BCRs associated with adverse outcomes. Seven unmappable autosomal-autosomal BCRs with breakpoints involving pericentromeric/heterochromatic regions may represent a low-risk group. We performed independent phenotype-aware and blinded interpretation, which accurately predicted benign outcomes (specificity = 100%) but demonstrated relatively low sensitivity for prediction of the clinical outcome in affected carriers (sensitivity = 45%–55%). This sensitivity emphasizes the challenges associated with prenatal risk prediction for long-term morbidity in the absence of phenotypic data given the still immature annotation of the morbidity genome and poorly understood long-range regulatory mechanisms. In conclusion, we upwardly revise the previous estimates of Warburton to a morbidity risk of 27% and recommend sequencing of the chromosomal breakpoints as the first-tier diagnostic test in pregnancies with a de novo BCR.","author_count":26,"contributors":["Halgren, Christina","Nielsen, Nete M.","Nazaryan-Petersen, Lusine","Silahtaroglu, Asli","Collins, Ryan L.","Lowther, Chelsea","Kjaergaard, Susanne","Frisch, Morten","Kirchhoff, Maria","Brøndum-Nielsen, Karen","Lind-Thomsen, Allan","Mang, Yuan","El-Schich, Zahra","Boring, Claire A.","Mehrjouy, Mana M.","Jensen, Peter K.A.","Fagerberg, Christina","Krogh, Lotte N.","Hansen, Jan","Bryndorf, Thue","Hansen, Claus","Talkowski, Michael E.","Bak, Mads","Tommerup, Niels","Bache, Iben"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-016972"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Bache, Iben"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Broad Institute","Harvard Medical School","Harvard University","Massachusetts General Hospital"],"keyword":["Chromosome Aberrations","Chromosome Breakpoints","Clinical recommendations","Cohort Studies","De novo","Evolution Molecular","Female","Genome Human","Humans","Inversion","Karyotyping","Long term follow up","Pregnancy","Prenatal Diagnosis","Prenatal Diagnosis methods","RNA Long Noncoding genetics","Risk Factors","Sequence Analysis DNA","Time Factors","balanced chromosomal rearrangement","mate pair mapping","morbidity risk","neurodevelopmental psychiatric disorders","reciprocal translocation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"risks and recommendations in prenatally detected de novo balanced chromosomal rearrangements from assessment of long term outcomes","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/151868134/main_2_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We thank the families who have participated in the clinical study and the Danish Cytogenetic Registry for providing data for the national cohort. We also thank Annemette F. Mikkelsen, Karen F. Henriksen, Bjarke Thomsen, Elisabeth Larsen, Theresa Wass, and Ingrid Kjær for technical assistance and Juan Manuel Medina Mendez for bioinformatic support. This work was supported by the Danish National Research Foundation ( DG48 ) and The Lundbeck Foundation ( 2013-14290 ) as well as the National Institutes of Health ( HD081256 and GM061354 to M.E.T.) and the March of Dimes ( 6-FY15-255 to M.E.T.)."],"id":"S03-100.00-016972","identifier":[{"id":"PMC5992120","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"29805044","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85047187634","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-3ac989e6-79b4-422c-ae3c-b9533476dd29","ku-8b6c5140-7aea-4a66-9eb1-1d604d045a2d","sdu-08ecac69-fde9-4139-b901-1aa493ad4c1d"],"keyword":["Chromosome Aberrations","Chromosome Breakpoints","Cohort Studies","Conserved Sequence/genetics","Evolution, Molecular","Female","Genome, Human","Humans","Karyotyping","Pregnancy","Prenatal Diagnosis/methods","RNA, Long Noncoding/genetics","Risk Factors","Sequence Analysis, DNA","Time Factors","balanced chromosomal rearrangement","clinical recommendations","de novo","inversion","long-term follow-up","mate-pair mapping","morbidity risk","neurodevelopmental/-psychiatric disorders","prenatal diagnosis","reciprocal translocation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-DD8C15E12DBC51CF700A325FC9BB0288"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5992120/pdf/main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/151868134/main_2_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Reali